#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069808} :activity/id #uuid "bea02a45-ef63-58e9-9f2c-b150acec23b9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050171 :topic/id #uuid "33d5c327-dce4-5785-8656-637262238322" :topic/math-academy-id 475} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069808 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070009 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we have the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx(t) = t+1, \\qquad y(t) = t^2+2, \\qquad t\\in (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\nThese parametric equations trace out a curve in the $xy$-plane. We can combine these into a single function, called a **vector-valued** function $\\mathbf r (t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r (t) = \\langle x(t), \\: y(t)\\rangle  =  \\langle t+1, \\: t^2+2\\rangle\n$$\n\nThe function $\\mathbf r (t)$ takes a **parameter** $t$ as input and returns a **position vector**. For a given value of $t,$ each value of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ has its tail at the origin and its terminal point at the coordinates \n$(t+1, \\: t^2+2).$\n\n![](images/10/1023d1a1898a402b95d8374f6242d494f714cde9c5ad4cb8186567b9ea192431.png)\n\nEvaluating vector-valued functions is straightforward. Just remember that the output is a vector, not a scalar. \n\nFor the vectors in the graph above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-3) & =⟨(-3)+1,\\,(-3)^{2}+2⟩=⟨-2,\\,11⟩, \\\\ r(0) & =⟨(0)+1,\\,(0)^{2}+2⟩=⟨1,\\,2⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨(2)+1,\\,(2)^{2}+2⟩=⟨3,\\,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5439d77a-b766-5dc3-ad68-6facc632d0f1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7721 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1f95d8fa-a5ab-52bb-954e-880438fde745" :step/math-academy-id 44768 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069809}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069809 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068857 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068457 :question/id #uuid "6eea01b2-bc2a-55a8-9bab-6789df00fc42" :question/math-academy-id "e-12450"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d59ccd52-63db-5d28-b78b-2df7caf52ad0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048293}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259489} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393473} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427593}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Vector-Valued Function"} :step/id #uuid "4e87da40-1bc7-5937-9854-566b24b722d7" :step/math-academy-id 44769 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069815}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069811 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070010 :tutorial/content "The **magnitude** of a vector-valued function \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left< x(t), \\: y(t) \\right>,\n$$\n\ndenoted $|\\mathbf{r}(t)|,$ is defined as follows:\n\n$$\n|\\mathbf{r}(t)| = \\sqrt{(x(t))^2+(y(t))^2}\n$$\n\nThe magnitude of a three-dimensional vector-valued function \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left< x(t), \\: y(t), \\: z(t) \\right>\n$$\n\nis defined similarly:\n\n$$\n|\\mathbf{r}(t)| = \\sqrt{(x(t))^2+(y(t))^2+(z(t))^2}\n$$\n\n**Note:** An alternative notation that's often used for the magnitude of a vector-valued function is $||\\mathbf r(t) ||.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1aaa056f-942b-569d-a4db-4051ddf994ae" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7722 :tutorial/title "The Magnitude of a Vector-Valued Function"} :step/id #uuid "2f3b3f68-570e-520e-95c8-902f36d55bbc" :step/math-academy-id 44772 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069812}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069812 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068858 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068458 :question/id #uuid "86e423e7-db4b-5fdf-a285-bded3bd8161e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12451"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e520dc67-3b63-5ca7-959b-f642d85529d6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051021}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319278} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345016} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345019} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372984}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Magnitude of a Vector-Valued Function"} :step/id #uuid "2e5ebb70-65ff-51e9-aa1f-59730dada6c1" :step/math-academy-id 44773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069813 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068859 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068459 :question/id #uuid "ab3899d3-8838-57ad-8a17-b8ac2934c0f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-12452"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26571ec1-e645-5f07-ba4e-75c22558394a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050173}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278337} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319274} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427592}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Three-Component Vector-Valued Function"} :step/id #uuid "063e1808-5b72-584e-a9c3-d3888f153383" :step/math-academy-id 44776 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069811}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069815 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070011 :tutorial/content "We can also define vector-valued functions with three components. For example,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f} (t) = t \\ \\mathbf{i} + 2t^2 \\ \\mathbf{j} + 3t^3 \\ \\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nThis particular definition uses the so-called $\\mathbf{i}$-$\\mathbf{j}$-$\\mathbf{k}$ notation.\n\nWe can represent the same function using **angle bracket** notation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f (t) = \\langle t,  \\: 2t^2, \\: 3t^3 \\rangle\n$$\n\nAnother notation that's often convenient is **bracket** notation:\n\n![](images/a9/a95fd21b6ea5e7eae150b516d7d0ad82d283a31c895c9223abbeac81b622bb7d.svg)\n\nIt's important to realize that all three notations represent the same function." :tutorial/id #uuid "93d7dd93-747b-51cf-b634-a766d1aef404" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7723 :tutorial/title "Three-Component Functions"} :step/id #uuid "677f9f3d-0e2b-5d85-b640-373f0553cba4" :step/math-academy-id 44780 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069813}}] :activity/title "Defining Vector-Valued Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068999}]}
